  "response": "The `crimes context` command failed on the fixture, but the scan output and the file itself tell the full story.\n\n**The finding:** `crime_00003` — \"Half-UTC, Half-Local\" — identifies the receiver `d` in `dateLabel()` (lines 40–41) as mixing UTC and local Date methods on the same variable.\n\n**The problem in `dateLabel()`:**\n\n```ts\nconst d = new Date();\nreturn d.getUTCFullYear() + \"-\" + d.getMonth() + \"-\" + d.getDate();\n```\n\n`d.getUTCFullYear()` reads the year in UTC, but `d.getMonth()` and `d.getDate()` read the month and day in the host's local timezone. Around midnight UTC, these can disagree — the UTC year might be 2027 while the local month/day are still in December 2026 (or vice versa).\n\n**Proposed refactor — pick UTC consistently:**\n\n```ts\nexport function dateLabel(): string {\n  const d = new Date();\n  const y = d.getUTCFullYear();\n  const m = String(d.getUTCMonth() + 1).padStart(2, \"0\");\n  const day = String(d.getUTCDate()).padStart(2, \"0\");\n  return `${y}-${m}-${day}`;\n}\n```\n\nThis fixes three bugs at once:\n1. **Mixed families** — all reads are now UTC\n2. **Zero-indexed month** — `getMonth()` / `getUTCMonth()` returns 0–11; the original didn't add 1 on the local call\n3. **Missing zero-padding** — months and days are now padded to two digits, producing ISO-style `YYYY-MM-DD`\n\nUTC is the right choice here because `todayKey()` (line 8) already uses the UTC family for the same purpose, so this aligns `dateLabel` with the rest of the file's storage-oriented functions. Local formatting belongs at the display boundary (like `prettyDueDate`), not in key/label generation.",
